WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:06.270
Ben Affleck's Batman will live on in the multiverse-twisting
"Flash" movie – but Matt Reeves' "The Batman"

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.680
is doing something new in a world all its
own.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.840
Here's everything you need to know about Robert
Pattinson and "The Batman."

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.250
"The Batman" has been in development for so
long that the Bat was originally supposed

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.690
to be a totally different man.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:24.320
Originally, "The Batman" was imagined as an
entry in the DC Extended Universe, working

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.700
in the same world as "Man of Steel" and "Batman
v. Superman: Dawn of Justice"

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.730
At the time, it was a given that Ben Affleck
would be reprising the role of Bruce Wayne,

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.530
while also serving as the movie's writer and
director.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:40.350
Once Affleck stepped down as director and
his script was shelved, new director Matt

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.940
Reeves came on board with his own pitch for
a Batman film that would call for a younger

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.350
actor in the role.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:49.079
Robert Pattinson was Reeves' first choice
for Bruce Wayne, with the filmmaker saying

00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:52.040
he wrote the part specifically for him.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.090
Pattinson is best known to the public for
his lead role in the "Twilight" films, but

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:59.270
in the years since those movies came and went,
he's also become a critical darling for his

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.989
work in smaller films like "Good Time" and
"The Lighthouse."

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:05.170
Pattinson's casting was something of a surprise.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:09.810
For years, he's avoided major franchise projects,
reportedly having a less-than-stellar experience

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:10.979
with "Twilight."

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:15.340
But it turns out that he, like a lot of people,
is a lifelong Batman fan, and jumped at the

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.359
chance to portray his own version of the character.

00:01:18.359 --> 00:01:23.189
Pattinson's casting in 2019 was met with a
predictable amount of fan backlash, and Pattinson

00:01:23.189 --> 00:01:26.990
also stirred up a bit of controversy when
he jokingly claimed he wouldn't be hitting

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:29.219
the gym to beef up for the role.

00:01:29.219 --> 00:01:33.249
This kind of criticism is nothing new, and
it's hard to take too seriously; remember

00:01:33.249 --> 00:01:36.999
that originally people had doubts about Heath
Ledger as the Joker.

00:01:36.999 --> 00:01:41.279
Other criticisms around his interpretation
haven't made a lot of sense, like mocking

00:01:41.279 --> 00:01:45.929
the so-called "emo" black makeup around Pattinson's
eyes when he removes his cowl.

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:50.039
Those critics are apparently unaware that
every live-action Batman since Michael Keaton

00:01:50.039 --> 00:01:54.340
has filled in the space between their eyes
and their cowl with black make-up … at least

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.919
when the cowl is on.

00:01:55.919 --> 00:02:00.859
Unlike the Caped Crusader in "Batman Returns"
and "Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice,"

00:02:00.859 --> 00:02:06.149
this Batman still has the make-up on when
he removes his cowl, which just makes sense.

00:02:06.149 --> 00:02:09.630
One thing's for certain: Pattinson's take
will certainly be different from what we've

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.620
seen on screen before – less charming, more
broken.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:13.950
The actor told MovieMaker,

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:18.860
"I want to play a dirty, dirty, slimy Batman."

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.110
Soon after Ben Affleck walked away from the
"Batman" directing gig, news broke that Matt

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:27.590
Reeves would be taking on the task of shepherding
"The Batman" from concept to completion.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:31.720
While "The Batman" will be Reeves' first foray
into the world of superheroes, he is no stranger

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:36.890
to films featuring high concepts and larger-than-life
characters, as evidenced by his directorial

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:40.350
work on "Cloverfield" and the rebooted "Planet
of the Apes" films.

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:44.900
At first, the intent was for Reeves to use
Ben Affleck's script, directing Affleck in

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:45.900
the role.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.260
But a few months after he was brought on board
as director, Reeves announced that he was

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.640
tossing out Affleck's screenplay and starting
from scratch.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.350
While Reeves' script features a younger protagonist
than Affleck's would have, the director told

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:02.030
Variety that he still hopes to deliver a mature,
ambitious film that doesn't talk down to its

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:06.110
audience and offers a quote, "epic and emotional
new take" on the character.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:11.540
When "The Batman" was first announced, it
was intended as another chapter in the DC

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:16.460
Extended Universe, pitting Ben Affleck's veteran
Dark Knight against the assassin Slade Wilson,

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:17.460
a.k.a.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.290
Deathstroke, to be played by Joe Manganiello.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.820
Manganiello's role in the film was teased
in Zack Snyder's version of "Justice League,"

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:26.970
which showed Lex Luthor revealing Batman's
secret identity to Deathstroke.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:31.580
"His name is Bruce Wayne."

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:35.730
According to Manganiello, Deathstroke would
have used this information to exact his cruel

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:40.310
vengeance on those close to Bruce Wayne, and
to do battle with both Batman and his teammate

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:41.340
Batgirl.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:44.980
This one of the many plot threads from "Zack
Snyder's Justice League" that are now left

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:49.710
dangling indefinitely as DC Films retools
its cinematic universe.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:53.390
After discarding the original script for "The
Batman," Matt Reeves set out to write a new

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:55.660
story that would stand more on its own.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:57.700
He said in a 2022 interview with Esquire,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:01.920
"I respect that the DC Universe has become
an extended universe and all the movies were

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.240
kind of connected.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.980
But another 'Batman' film, it shouldn’t
have to carry the weight of connecting the

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:08.750
characters from all those other movies.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:10.130
I didn’t want them in there."

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:15.220
Instead, "The Batman" would be set in its
own continuity, divorced from the DCEU.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:19.439
But fans itching for more for more Batfleck
will still get at least one final film featuring

00:04:19.439 --> 00:04:24.490
his take on the character, when "The Flash"
hits theaters in November 2022.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:28.560
In an interview with the Hollywood Reporter
in 2019, Reeves said that his screenplay for

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.469
"The Batman" would be a, quote, "point of
view-driven, noir Batman tale."

00:04:32.469 --> 00:04:36.870
He also said it would lean into the notion
of Bruce Wayne as the world's greatest detective.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:40.439
Reeves also promised an emotional, transformative
arc for the character.

00:04:40.439 --> 00:04:44.719
Reeves told Digital Spy that he sees Batman
as a, quote, "tortured soul who is struggling

00:04:44.719 --> 00:04:48.139
to find a way to do the right thing in a very
imperfect world."

00:04:48.139 --> 00:04:52.009
As Christian Bale's Batman famously said in
"Batman Begins,"

00:04:52.009 --> 00:04:59.129
"It's not who I am underneath, but what I
do that defines me."

00:04:59.129 --> 00:05:02.830
Based on Reeves' comments, we expect his take
on the character to be more introspective

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:05.580
and intellectual than what we've seen in previous
films.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.309
While it's probably a safe bet that "The Batman"
will feature its fair share of fistfights,

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:15.490
explosions, and mind-blowing Bat-gadgets — after
all, this is still a DC superhero movie — don't

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:21.770
be surprised if Reeves' Batman turns out to
be defined by who he is underneath after all.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:25.539
It was revealed early on that key villain
roles in "The Batman" would be filled by the

00:05:25.539 --> 00:05:27.409
Penguin and Catwoman.

00:05:27.409 --> 00:05:31.930
After several rounds of lively casting rumors
that included Vanessa Hudgens, Aubrey Plaza,

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:37.699
Lupita Nyong'o, Tessa Thompson, Gugu Mbatha-Raw,
Alexandra Shipp, and Logan Browning, speculation

00:05:37.699 --> 00:05:42.150
about who will be portraying Selina Kyle in
"The Batman" finally came to an end.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:46.759
"Big Little Lies" star Zoë Kravitz will be
portraying the Caped Crusader's iconic femme

00:05:46.759 --> 00:05:48.419
fatale in Matt Reeves' film.

00:05:48.419 --> 00:05:52.080
Selina has a rich and complex history with
Bruce Wayne in the comics, and her role in

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.169
the film could go any number of directions.

00:05:54.169 --> 00:05:58.590
"The Batman" won't be Kravitz's first or even
second foray into comic book fare, although

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:01.650
it'll be her first starring role in a superhero
film.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:04.809
She previously played the mutant Angel Salvadore,
a.k.a.

00:06:04.809 --> 00:06:10.860
Tempest, in 2011's "X-Men: First Class," and
also provided the voice for Mary Jane in 2018's

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.710
"Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse."

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:15.589
The most purr-fectly relevant gig on her resume?

00:06:15.589 --> 00:06:19.590
She's already played Catwoman once before,
having lent her voice to "The LEGO Batman

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:20.590
Movie."

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:26.059
While Catwoman has rarely been depicted as
an outright villain in Batman mythology, the

00:06:26.059 --> 00:06:31.099
past 30 years in particular have seen her
rise into an anti-hero in her own right.

00:06:31.099 --> 00:06:35.999
In the comics, Selina Kyle has led her own
ongoing comics series almost continuously

00:06:35.999 --> 00:06:39.999
since 1993, after a limited series run in
1989.

00:06:39.999 --> 00:06:45.529
She's arguably the protagonist of 1992's "Batman
Returns," got a much-mocked film of her own

00:06:45.529 --> 00:06:49.879
in 2004, and fought alongside Batman in 2012's
"The Dark Knight Rises."

00:06:49.879 --> 00:06:54.110
If the trailers are any indication, "The Batman"
will explore the character from a few different

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:59.050
angles — as a thief, as a vigilante, and
as a romantic foil for Batman.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:03.379
Zoë Kravitz told Empire magazine that "The
Batman" is "an origin story for Selina."

00:07:03.379 --> 00:07:07.699
In keeping with Matt Reeves' vision of "The
Batman" as a film noir, Kravitz sees Selina

00:07:07.699 --> 00:07:10.389
as a femme fatale, as well as a fighter.

00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:13.990
Kravitz studied a mix of different martial
arts that would be practical for a smaller,

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:17.490
leaner person to use when taking on larger
opponents.

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:22.160
Selina's severe haircut and makeshift Catwoman
disguise seen in the film seem heavily inspired

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:26.759
by her appearance in the famous four-issue
arc "Batman: Year One," by writer Frank Miller

00:07:26.759 --> 00:07:28.660
and artist David Mazzucchelli.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:33.539
In this story, Selina follows Batman's example
and creates a costumed identity under which

00:07:33.539 --> 00:07:37.749
to stalk the roofs of Gotham, but she and
Batman don't have much interaction.

00:07:37.749 --> 00:07:42.650
The early flirtation between Bat and Cat is
explored more fully in its spiritual sequel,

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:47.719
"The Long Halloween" by Jeph Loeb and Tim
Sale, which has been cited along with "Year

00:07:47.719 --> 00:07:50.879
One" as one of the inspirations for "The Batman."

00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:55.229
The path "The Batman" took from announcement
to release spanned nearly eight years.

00:07:55.229 --> 00:07:59.399
It was among the slate of 10 DC Comics-inspired
films that Warner Bros. announced way back

00:07:59.399 --> 00:08:05.719
in October 2014, before cameras had even stopped
rolling on "Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice."

00:08:05.719 --> 00:08:10.399
The Matt Reeves "Batman" was initially supposed
to be released on June 25, 2021, but this

00:08:10.399 --> 00:08:14.669
announcement was made before the start of
the COVID-19 pandemic, which halted production

00:08:14.669 --> 00:08:17.000
about a quarter of the way through shooting.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:22.360
This pushed the release to October 1, 2021,
but an outbreak of the virus on set in September

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:25.819
2020 paused production for an additional two
weeks.

00:08:25.819 --> 00:08:29.710
This may have contributed to the release date
being moved yet again, though it's more likely

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:34.030
that Warner Bros. simply wanted a few more
months for cases of the disease to decline

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:37.720
… and, perhaps, to not have to release the
film day-and-date on HBO Max with the rest

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:39.520
of their 2021 slate.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.200
Shortly after the second COVID delay, "The
Batman" received its third and final release

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.390
date of March 4, 2022.

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:50.510
The film will play exclusively in theaters
for 45 days, and then stream on HBO Max beginning

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:51.710
April 19.

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:56.410
"The Batman" finds Bruce Wayne in less than
ideal condition, and we're not just talking

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:57.900
about those scars on his back.

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:02.760
A year into his crimefighting career as Batman,
Wayne is still struggling with some deep psychological

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:06.770
scars and allowing his life outside the cape
and cowl to crumble.

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:10.350
Writer-director Matt Reeves has stated in
an interview with Empire that this version

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:14.810
of Bruce Wayne is "not really a socialite
at all" and is letting his family home and

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:16.500
legacy decay.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:19.750
Reeves' inspiration for the depressed, rage-fueled
Bruce Wayne?

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.510
Tragic '90s rock icon Kurt Cobain.

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:26.880
According to his feature in Esquire, Reeves
became fixated on the Nirvana song "Something

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:30.781
in the Way" while writing the first act of
the script, leading him to imagine Bruce Wayne

00:09:30.781 --> 00:09:35.190
as a total recluse struggling with addiction
— in his case, to violence.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:39.370
He cites the song, which was remixed for film's
first theatrical trailer, and the Gus Van

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:44.220
Sant film "Last Days," which centered around
a Kurt Cobain-type character, as inspiring

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:45.400
this take on Bruce.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:50.770
According to Pattinson, his Batman is constantly
battling against his own urge to kill, displacing

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:55.280
the rage he feels towards his parents' killer
onto the other criminals of Gotham.

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:59.150
Being Batman is an outlet for his anger, but
not necessarily a salve.

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:01.780
In short, he's not getting better.

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:05.970
Early in the development of "The Batman,"
Matt Reeves parried speculation that his "young

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:08.520
Batman" movie would be yet another origin
story.

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:13.150
Instead, Reeves has defined his film as a
"Year Two" Batman tale, skipping past the

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:17.060
well-trod territory of "Batman Begins" and
"Batman: Year One."

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:18.060
Reeves told Esquire,

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:22.700
"A lot of times you see stories where he's
already become Batman, then the Rogues' Gallery

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:27.170
villain comes in, and it's then their story,
and you watch him go toe-to-toe with them.

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:31.820
I wanted the main character in the story to
be a Batman who was a year in and still trying

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:37.010
to figure out how to do this, how to be effective,
and he's not necessarily succeeding."

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:41.700
Any time a filmmaker comes on board to "reboot"
the Batman character for the silver screen,

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:46.550
you'll hear the same familiar refrain — they
want to take the character back to his gritty,

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:47.550
pulpy roots.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:51.570
Usually, this means looking back at one of
two essential graphic novels of the mid-1980s,

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:55.890
"The Dark Knight Returns" and "Batman: Year
One," both written by Frank Miller, which

00:10:55.890 --> 00:11:00.110
helped codify a more grounded take that had
been evolving for decades in the pages of

00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:01.240
the monthly comics.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:05.360
Matt Reeves is certainly not an exception
to this — "Year One" is indeed one of his

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:06.360
favorites.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:11.590
He's also cited Jeph Loeb and Tim Sale's 1997
mini-series "The Long Halloween" as an inspiration.

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:15.870
In fact, at one point Warner Bros. Animation
was told to put their own adaptation of the

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:19.700
"Long Halloween" story on hold because it
was being considered as the direct source

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:21.070
material for "The Batman."

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:24.880
It wouldn't be the first time the comic served
as inspiration for a Batman film, either;

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:29.780
"The Long Halloween" is one of the clearest
comics influences on 2008's "The Dark Knight."

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:34.600
More exciting, however, is Reeves' professed
love of not only pre-Miller Batman creators

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:39.700
like Bill Finger and Neal Adams, but the 2000
graphic novel "Batman: Ego" by cartoonist

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:40.800
Darwyn Cooke.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:45.780
"Ego" is a deep and visually stunning psychological
study of the Dark Knight, perfect inspiration

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:50.890
for a Batman film that's intended to be richly
emotional and focused on the title character.

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:55.100
The trailers also hint that certain aspects
of Scott Snyder and Greg Capullo's run on

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:58.410
the character may make an appearance as well.

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:02.440
Character actor Jeffrey Wright becomes the
fifth man to portray Gotham City Police Commissioner

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:07.811
James Gordon in a live-action feature, following
Neil Hamilton in the 1966 continuation of

00:12:07.811 --> 00:12:12.430
the Batman television series, Pat Hingle in
the Burton and Schumacher series, Gary Oldman

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:14.240
in the "Dark Knight" trilogy, and J.K.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:16.690
Simmons in the DC Extended Universe.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:21.740
Wright is no stranger to major franchises,
having played CIA Agent Felix Leiter in three

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:26.240
installments of the James Bond series, Beetee
in "The Hunger Games: Mockingjay," and having

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:29.950
lent his voice to Uatu the Watcher in "Marvel's
What If?"

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:33.540
In the years since his casting in "The Batman,"
Jeffrey Wright has also lent his voice to

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:38.250
the Dark Knight himself in "Batman: The Audio
Adventures," a campy radio-style drama that

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:39.990
streams on HBO Max.

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:43.550
According to an interview with IndieWire,
Wright was attracted to the realistic tone

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:44.550
of the film.

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:48.840
"I saw the themes around corruption and class
tensions and all of the things that he wanted

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:53.120
to bring from the outside into this world
so it had some relevance."

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:57.850
Warner Bros. confirmed in October 2019 that
Paul Dano had officially come aboard the film

00:12:57.850 --> 00:12:58.930
as the Riddler.

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:03.120
Dano is well suited for a dark and menacing
villain, having been nominated for an Emmy

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:07.600
for his work on the Showtime limited series
"Escape at Dannemora," in which he portrayed

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:10.470
real-life convicted murderer David Sweat.

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:15.520
Dano has played a wide variety of roles during
his eclectic career, from gently comedic turns

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:20.290
in films like "Swiss Army Man" and "Little
Miss Sunshine," to more serious roles in films

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:23.070
such as "Prisoners," "Okja," and "There Will
Be Blood."

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:27.970
The thread that seems to tie all of Dano's
characters together is a sense of deep interior

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:33.030
personality and emotional complexity, which
will serve him well as the enigmatic Riddler.

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:37.750
Matt Reeves' casting of the soulful, often
somber Dano is a far cry from the rubber-limbed

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:41.240
Jim Carrey, who played the role in 1995's
"Batman Forever."

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:45.650
"Post-homicidal depression."

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:49.390
When Matt Reeves imagined his ideal Batman
film, it was always through the lens of a

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:50.910
detective story.

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:55.180
From there, it was a logical leap for the
film's main antagonist to be more of an intellectual

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:57.550
challenge for Batman than a physical threat.

00:13:57.550 --> 00:14:01.630
Hence, the Riddler, a Batman villain famous
for his puzzles and mind games rather than

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:03.090
fighting prowess.

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:07.470
In "The Batman," Matt Reeves has reimagined
the Riddler as a serial killer in the tradition

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:11.920
of the Zodiac Killer, the real-life murderer
who mailed clues about his targets and identity

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:14.720
to newspapers in the late 1960s.

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:15.720
Reeves told Esquire,

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:19.730
"I started thinking of the Zodiac Killer,
because he did create a costume for himself

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:22.900
and he wore a black hood, he had his own insignia.

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:27.160
He was an early anti-superhero, a scary figure
who terrorized California."

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:32.210
This version of the Riddler, who uses his
canonical birth name from the comics, Edward

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:36.940
Nashton, taunts Batman with written and recorded
messages, and may even already be aware of

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:39.030
his secret identity as Bruce Wayne.

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:43.470
Indeed, Riddler seems to be directing Batman
toward solving a mystery about corruption

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:45.990
in Gotham that involves the Wayne family.

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:51.240
Without question the most surprising casting
choice in "The Batman" is Colin Farrell as

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:52.460
Oswald "Penguin" Cobblepot.

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:57.900
Cobblepot is typically portrayed as a short,
ugly man with a rotund body, a far cry from

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:00.100
Colin Farrell's leading-man good looks.

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:05.170
But, like Danny Devito, and, to a lesser extent,
"Gotham" actor Robin Lord Taylor, before him,

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:09.050
Farrell's appearance has been altered via
applied prosthetics to make him a closer match

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:11.400
for the notorious crime boss of the comics.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:16.080
It took between two and four hours to apply
Farrell's facial scars, a heftier neck, and

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:19.540
receding hairline, though his transformation
doesn't stop there.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:23.370
Farrell is practically unrecognizable in his
performance, donning the type of thick New

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:26.630
York accent you'd hear in a Scorsese gangster
film.

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:29.540
"Woah, take it easy sweetheart."

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:33.880
Or should we say Coppola — Farrell cites
"The Godfather" character Fredo Corleone,

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:38.840
the weak-willed older brother of kingpin Michael
Corleone, as his inspiration for the character.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:42.950
Like Fredo, Farrell told Empire Online that
his "Oz" Cobblepot is, quote "crippled by

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:47.400
the insignificance that he lives within, in
a family that is full of very strong, very

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:49.910
bright, very capable, very violent men."

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:54.050
It's a role he says he'd be interested in
exploring in a sequel, and he may very well

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:57.300
get the chance to spend more time with the
character — there's a spinoff series for

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:00.470
the Penguin in early development at HBO Max.

00:16:00.470 --> 00:16:04.910
In the comics, Oswald comes from old Gotham
money, operating his criminal empire under

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:07.430
the very thin guise of legitimate business.

00:16:07.430 --> 00:16:11.630
He's often portrayed as one of the only "traditional"
gangsters to maintain his foothold in the

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:16.670
Gotham underworld after the rise of more outlandish
villains like the Joker, Two-Face, and Black

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:17.670
Mask.

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:22.250
Whether Oz's inferiority complex may come
from feeling overshadowed by his family legacy,

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:27.470
or from the changing face of crime in Gotham
in "The Batman," we'll just have to wait and

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:28.470
see.

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:32.730
The impressive cast of "The Batman" also includes
Emmy-winning character actor John Turturro,

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:36.600
who will be playing Gotham's powerful crime
boss Carmine Falcone.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:41.310
Sometimes nicknamed "The Roman" and based
on Marlon Brando's character from "The Godfather,"

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:46.050
Falcone first debuted in Frank Miller and
David Mazzucchelli's"Batman: Year One" comics,

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:50.100
but assumed a much more prominent role later
on in "Batman: The Long Halloween."

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:54.930
In the comics, Falcone effectively runs Gotham
City and is determined to put a stop to Batman's

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:58.730
vigilante actions in order to solidify his
stranglehold on the city.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:03.470
Falcone has previously been portrayed onscreen
by Tom Wilkinson in the film "Batman Begins"

00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:06.620
and John Doman on the television series "Gotham."

00:17:06.620 --> 00:17:10.380
Further complicating Falcone's relationship
with Batman in the comics is the fact that

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:16.000
Bruce's father, Thomas Wayne, once saved Falcone's
life, a life debt which Falcone has sworn

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:18.360
to someday repay.

00:17:18.360 --> 00:17:22.689
Andy Serkis, the actor best known for his
groundbreaking CGI "performance capture" roles

00:17:22.689 --> 00:17:27.280
as Gollum in "The Lord of the Rings" and Caesar
in the rebooted "Planet of the Apes" trilogy,

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:31.800
will show his actual face in "The Batman"
as Alfred Pennyworth, the Wayne family butler

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:34.930
who raised the young Bruce after the murder
of his parents.

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:39.440
Serkis becomes the fifth actor to play Alfred
in a live-action feature film, following Alan

00:17:39.440 --> 00:17:43.340
Napier, Michael Gough, Michael Caine, and
Jeremy Irons.

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:46.809
The character has also been a fixture on the
small screen in recent years, portrayed by

00:17:46.809 --> 00:17:52.710
Sean Pertwee on "Gotham" from 2014 to 2019
and the lead character of his own series,

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:55.620
"Pennyworth," starring Jack Bannon, on HBO
Max.

00:17:55.620 --> 00:17:59.280
Serkis has plenty of history with Matt Reeves,
who also directed "Dawn of the Planet of the

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:03.380
Apes" and "War for the Planet of the Apes,"
and Reeves has apparently secured Serkis a

00:18:03.380 --> 00:18:05.320
sizable role in "The Batman."

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:09.600
Serkis has told interviewers that the emotional
connection between Alfred and Bruce is "the

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:13.880
center" of the film, and trailers have hinted
that the story may hinge on some Wayne family

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:17.540
secret from which Alfred has been trying to
protect his surrogate son.

00:18:17.540 --> 00:18:21.059
A larger role for Alfred in this film would
be in keeping with the way his character has

00:18:21.059 --> 00:18:25.530
been expanded in the comics over the past
few decades, from a relatively passive participant

00:18:25.530 --> 00:18:30.280
in Batman's war on crime to an irreplaceable
asset with a history as a military medic and

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:33.059
a secret agent of the British government.

00:18:33.059 --> 00:18:36.930
Particularly during Batman's early years before
the expansion of the Bat-family, Alfred is

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:40.300
Bruce's only friend and confidante.

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:44.470
Similarly to the way Matt Reeves announced
the casting for Andy Serkis as Alfred, the

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:49.150
director took to Twitter again at the beginning
of December 2019 to reveal that Peter Sarsgaard

00:18:49.150 --> 00:18:51.180
had joined the cast of "The Batman."

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:55.040
Unlike Reeves' previous few casting announcements,
he didn't hashtag this tweet with the name

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:59.530
of the character Sarsgaard will be playing,
instead just sharing an animated gif.

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:03.250
Sources originally told The Hollywood Reporter
that the actor would either be playing a corrupt

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:07.890
cop named Wasserman or a district attorney,
leading to speculation that he could be playing

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:11.180
Harvey Dent — better known as the iconic
Batman villain Two-Face.

00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:16.640
However, in early 2020, a Warner Bros. official
press release identified Sarsgaard's character

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:17.900
as "Gotham D.A.

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:21.550
Gil Colson," a new character who doesn't exist
in the comics.

00:19:21.550 --> 00:19:25.660
The character has already made an appearance
in a promo clip, being used as a hostage by

00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:28.120
the Riddler during a brazen attack on a funeral.

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:29.280
"Christ, it's Colson."

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:34.380
On February 13, 2020, Matt Reeves gave us
all an early Valentine's Day gift in the form

00:19:34.380 --> 00:19:39.120
of a red-tinted first look at Robert Pattinson
as the caped crusader.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:43.960
The 55-second camera test features Pattinson
in the full Batsuit and cowl, emerging from

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:46.770
the darkness into a sharp, crimson close-up.

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:50.990
Even this partial glimpse let fans know that
Pattinson's Batman would have a very different

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:53.290
look than the Dark Knights that have come
before.

00:19:53.290 --> 00:19:58.100
But what really sells the look isn't any particular
alchemy of materials or cut, but Pattinson's

00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:02.120
distinctive face, which becomes the ultimate
focus of the short video.

00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:05.929
Like Ben Affleck and Christian Bale before
him, Pattinson is gifted with an authentic

00:20:05.929 --> 00:20:10.670
Bruce Wayne jawline, cutting a silhouette
so recognizable that it makes you really wonder

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:14.490
why he thinks he can get away with only covering
up half his face.

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:18.940
On March 4, 2020, Matt Reeves tweeted out
the first look at the new Batmobile.

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:23.500
Gone is the bulky tank-like Tumbler of Christopher
Nolan's "Dark Knight" trilogy, as well as

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:28.111
the almost comically tricked-out stealth-bomber-meets-"Fury-Road"
version of Batman's ride that Ben Affleck

00:20:28.111 --> 00:20:29.530
drove in "Justice League."

00:20:29.530 --> 00:20:33.640
For "The Batman," Robert Pattinson will be
driving a reimagined Batmobile that looks

00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:37.790
like it could've been driven straight out
of a high-end garage, as opposed to a high-tech

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:40.580
research facility, a la Batman Begins.

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:45.210
This new take on Bruce Wayne's iconic transport
leans much more toward a classic muscle car

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:50.540
than its more military-inspired predecessors,
calling to mind well-known models like the

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:52.860
1970 Dodge Challenger and the Pontiac GTO.

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:58.080
Since "The Batman" is supposed to take place
early on in Bruce's tenure as the Caped Crusader,

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:01.780
it's easy to believe that this version of
the Batmobile represents one of the billionaire

00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:05.890
vigilante's first attempts at a superhero
car, in which he modified something he already

00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:08.350
owned rather than building something new.

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:12.860
The latest version of the Batmobile comes
with a before-its-time in-dash video display,

00:21:12.860 --> 00:21:17.280
a sleek black custom design, and an absolute
monster of an engine.

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:22.300
"The Batman" is deliberately isolated from
the DC Extended Universe where most of DC's

00:21:22.300 --> 00:21:25.470
films take place, and is intended to stand
alone.

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:30.390
It neither relies on context from previous
film incarnations of Batman, nor does it prioritize

00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:32.610
setting up sequels and spinoffs.

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:36.860
Star Robert Pattinson seemed to imply to Total
Film after seeing the finished cut that he

00:21:36.860 --> 00:21:40.650
had a hard time imagining the movie leading
to a full film series – even though he admits

00:21:40.650 --> 00:21:41.950
it probably will.

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:42.950
As he put it,

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:47.530
"It’s a really, really unusual Batman story,
and it almost seems harder for me to imagine

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:49.030
it being a series afterwards.

00:21:49.030 --> 00:21:53.430
I mean, they always have that little bit at
the end, that’s like: ‘and coming up!’

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:55.520
But other than that, it feels strangely personal.

00:21:55.520 --> 00:21:58.580
I think people will be quite shocked at how
different it is."

00:21:58.580 --> 00:22:02.600
At the same time, it's been reported that
Warner Bros. is champing at the bit to spin

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:07.000
"The Batman" into a shared universe of films
and television shows, having already put two

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:10.380
HBO Max series into development ahead of the
film's release.

00:22:10.380 --> 00:22:14.780
One would center around the Gotham City Police
Department, while another would focus on Colin

00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:15.850
Farrell's Penguin.

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:20.740
This cross-platform Batman universe would
have to coexist alongside a few other live-action

00:22:20.740 --> 00:22:24.550
projects set in their own continuities, but
this is nothing new.

00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:30.110
The HBO Max series "Titans" and The CW's "Batwoman"
both feature a number of Batman characters,

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:32.600
take place on different Earths in the DC Multiverse.

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:37.480
Each show even has its own Bruce Wayne, played
by Iain Glen and Warren Christie respectively,

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:40.510
though both series avoid showing Batman in
costume.

00:22:40.510 --> 00:22:44.800
And then, of course, there's the DC Extended
Universe of films, which in 2022 alone will

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:48.820
feature both the Ben Affleck and Michael Keaton
incarnations of Batman.

00:22:48.820 --> 00:22:53.170
But even though Batman as a character is going
multiversal, on the big screen, it looks like

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:55.490
"The Batman" is set to stand alone.

